How to Handle Terraform Drift :- 🚨 

Terraform drift happens when your actual cloud infrastructure no longer matches what’s defined in your Terraform code.

This usually occurs when someone makes manual changes in the console or via CLI, bypassing Terraform.

Basic Example scenario:
 You created an S3 bucket using Terraform.
 Later, someone manually added a bucket policy directly in AWS.
 Now, Terraform’s state and the actual AWS configuration are out of sync.

This creates a gap between your code and your actual environment — leading to security risks, operational issues, and unpredictable deployments.

Steps to Handle Terraform Drift:- 

1️⃣ Detect the drift
 
terraform plan

This will show differences between your Terraform state and real infrastructure.

2️⃣ Decide what to do

If the change is valid and needed → Add it to your Terraform code so it’s tracked going forward.

If the change is not required → Remove it by running:

terraform apply

3️⃣ Prevent drift from happening

Restrict manual changes with IAM policies.

Enable drift detection using AWS Config rules or other monitoring tools.
Enforce Infrastructure as Code — treat Terraform as the only source of truth for provisioning and configuration.

Why this matters:
Terraform drift, if left unchecked, can lead to:

Unpredictable environments
Security misconfigurations
Deployment failures
Compliance issues

By detecting, deciding, and preventing drift, you ensure your infrastructure remains consistent, secure, and predictable.
